  "annotations_dropped": [
    {
      "annotation_id": "a571cae23d734bb7b6880840551fee03_10",
      "summary_section": "ANALYSIS",
      "labels": [
        "PRE_RELIED"
      ],
      "reasons": [
        "forbidden_section:ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "matched_phrases": [],
      "text_preview": "The only thing against the consent under Section 20 of the Act was that incorrect rule was mentioned in the order and that issue was admittedly directly covered by judgment of this Court in State of Gujarat v. Imtiyaz Haji Abdul Sattar [199"
    },
    {
      "annotation_id": "feec84452f05477093e1a1fddeaa2c7d_11",
      "summary_section": "ANALYSIS",
      "labels": [
        "PRE_RELIED"
      ],
      "reasons": [
        "forbidden_section:ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "matched_phrases": [],
      "text_preview": "The consent envisaged in the provisions of Section 20 of the Act as condition precedent to prosecution is a written consent for instituting the prosecution under the Act and not a consent for prosecuting the accused for a specific offence."
    },
    {
      "annotation_id": "babb0a41375745799c0bdc4aa2f4c52d_12",
      "summary_section": "ANALYSIS",
      "labels": [
        "ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "reasons": [
        "forbidden_section:ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "matched_phrases": [],
      "text_preview": "However, in the facts of the present case, the order granting consent (Ex.50) clearly mentioned Sections 2(ix)(k) and Rule 32(e) in the last column of the form indicating the alleged offence and also recorded discussion with the complainant"
    },
    {
      "annotation_id": "85647b309c4e4fd994800a1ec5683cc3_13",
      "summary_section": "ANALYSIS",
      "labels": [
        "ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "reasons": [
        "forbidden_section:ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "matched_phrases": [],
      "text_preview": "Apart from that, it may be erroneous to elevate the status of consent to that of sanction and defeat the prosecution, in view of the spirit and purpose of the Act as also the spirit and purpose of the provisions of Section 465 of Cr.P.C., b"
    },
    {
      "annotation_id": "ba37394256e44ba8a89592f8181b500d_14",
      "summary_section": "ANALYSIS",
      "labels": [
        "PRE_RELIED"
      ],
      "reasons": [
        "forbidden_section:ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "matched_phrases": [],
      "text_preview": "Reference in that regard may be had to State of Gujarat Vs. Chandraprakash K. Sindhi [1999 F.A.J 383]."
    },
    {
      "annotation_id": "ee7403948ed04d1481958993401a5e78_15",
      "summary_section": "ANALYSIS",
      "labels": [
        "PRE_RELIED"
      ],
      "reasons": [
        "forbidden_section:ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "matched_phrases": [],
      "text_preview": "Any lapse, error or omission in mentioning the Section or Rule for the violation of which prosecution was authorized could not be a ground for acquitting the accused person."
    },
    {
      "annotation_id": "0757e847979d4236bbd60d8e76adade0_16",
      "summary_section": "ANALYSIS",
      "labels": [
        "PRE_RELIED"
      ],
      "reasons": [
        "forbidden_section:ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "matched_phrases": [],
      "text_preview": "\nTherefore, the impugned judgment is illegal and erroneous to that extent.\"\n4."
    },
    {
      "annotation_id": "4f7bc4f5312946aa97f9714873cc9d6c_21",
      "summary_section": "ANALYSIS",
      "labels": [
        "RATIO"
      ],
      "reasons": [
        "forbidden_section:ANALYSIS"
      ],
      "matched_phrases": [],
      "text_preview": "However only because it refers section 27(1) of the FSS Act, 2006, the learned trial court has acquitted the respondent-accused without any cogent reasons."
    },
    {
      "annotation_id": "f9b191e352cb48a3a178360828fe3519_22",
